<BaseLayout title="Infotainment">
  <h1>Infotainment</h1>
  <p>The 2021 S60 uses Volvo's Sensus-era center display and driver information systems. Screen size, audio hardware, navigation, head-up display, and connected services depend on installed options and market. Available functions depend on installed equipment, market, phone compatibility, and software version.</p>

  <h2>Displays and controls</h2>
  <figure class="reference-photo"><img src="https://www.volvocars.com/media/support-content/img028459637a80b16dc0a801526df36585_1_--_--_VOICEpnghigh.png" alt="Volvo Sensus-era cockpit showing driver display, steering wheel and center touchscreen" loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Volvo Sensus overview image. Optional display equipment varies by vehicle.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>Volvo's Sensus interface uses a center touchscreen, a digital driver display, and an optional head-up display. Not every vehicle has every display option. See <a href="https://www.volvocars.com/uk/support/car/s60/2020/article/f6848b197ef39a36c0a80151207886f7/">Volvo's Sensus overview</a> for the system layout and feature descriptions.</p>

  <h2>Center display views</h2>
  <p>The Sensus center display has five main views. On a left-hand-drive car, swipe from Home view to reach App view on the right and Function view on the left; right-hand-drive layouts are reversed.</p>
  <table><thead><tr><th>View</th><th>Purpose</th><th>Typical use</th></tr></thead><tbody>
    <tr><th>Home</th><td>Four tiles for Navigation, Media, Phone and the most recently used other app/function.</td><td>Return to a familiar overview and expand a tile.</td></tr>
    <tr><th>Top</th><td>Settings, Owner's Manual, driver profile and stored messages.</td><td>Change settings or open context-sensitive help.</td></tr>
    <tr><th>Climate</th><td>Climate controls and additional heating/cooling settings.</td><td>Adjust temperature, fan, defrost and equipped seat heating.</td></tr>
    <tr><th>App</th><td>Installed vehicle and downloaded apps.</td><td>Open radio, media, phone integration and supported services.</td></tr>
    <tr><th>Function</th><td>Vehicle-function buttons.</td><td>Toggle equipped features such as BLIS, Lane Keeping Aid or Park Assist.</td></tr>
  </tbody></table>
  <p>Some tiles and buttons are equipment-dependent. A button shown in a generic manual image may not be installed on every car.</p>
  <h2>Settings categories</h2>
  <p>Volvo groups settings under My Car, Sound, Navigation, Media, Communication, Climate and System. Examples include display themes, driver-support settings, sound balance, navigation guidance, Bluetooth devices, phone/text settings, language and units, privacy/data sharing, and driver profiles. Menu labels can change with software and market.</p>
  <h2>Driver profiles</h2>
  <p>Many preferences can be stored in a driver profile, including display, mirror, seat, navigation, audio/media, language and voice-control settings. A key can be linked to a profile so the vehicle loads that profile when the key is used. Some settings are global and remain the same across profiles. If a setting seems to revert, check which profile is active and whether the profile is protected.</p>
  <h2>Phone connection and USB</h2>
  <p>Bluetooth pairing and phone projection are separate functions. For USB audio or phone integration, use the correct front USB port and a data-capable cable. Volvo's 2021 support information identifies the white-framed front USB port as the connection for Apple CarPlay and Android Auto when equipped; rear USB-C ports are for charging and do not play media through the vehicle system.</p>
  <p>Apple CarPlay requires a compatible iPhone and Siri enabled. Volvo's support instructions note that CarPlay cannot be used while the phone is connected to the car over Bluetooth. Android Auto availability depends on the installed USB hub and market; do not assume every S60 has the required two-port hub.</p>
  <h2>Internet and connected services</h2>
  <p>Depending on equipment and subscription, the vehicle may connect through Bluetooth, Wi-Fi or its integrated modem. Internet access can support web radio/music apps, software downloads and connected services. Data usage, roaming and service availability can depend on carrier, market and subscription status. If an app or service fails, check the active driver profile's data-sharing permissions before resetting the system.</p>
  <h2>Center display</h2>
  <p>The center display provides access to vehicle settings, media, phone functions, climate controls, and supported connected features. Learn the Home, App, Function, Top and Climate views while parked. Avoid navigating menus while the vehicle is moving.</p>

  <h2>Phone connection</h2>
  <p>For Bluetooth pairing, make the phone discoverable, open the vehicle's phone or Bluetooth settings, and follow the pairing prompts on both devices. Confirm the pairing code on both screens when requested. Menu wording may differ by software version.</p>

  <h2>Apple CarPlay and Android Auto</h2>
  <p>Phone integration availability and connection requirements should be checked against the equipment fitted to this particular car and the current phone software. Use a known-good data-capable USB cable when a wired connection is required; a charge-only cable will not carry data.</p>

  <h2>Audio and media</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Record the audio system name and speaker configuration from the vehicle's equipment/build information.</li>
    <li>Distinguish radio reception, Bluetooth audio, USB media, and phone-projection issues when troubleshooting.</li>
    <li>Check source volume and mute state before changing system settings.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Software and troubleshooting notes</h2>
  <p>When documenting a display issue, record the software version, phone model and operating-system version, connection method, selected media source, and whether the issue repeats after a vehicle restart. Do not assume that a factory reset is the first or safest troubleshooting step.</p>

